# Singer Ten light car, 1912-1924.
#
# The Singer Ten, launched at the Cycle and Motor Cycle Show in November 1912
# and in production to 1924. The 1919 open two-seater on registration AN 4354
# is the car modelled.
#
# Length 3.20 m, wheelbase 2.438 m (the post-1916 figure; it was 2.286 m at
# launch). Source: Grant Ford's Singer 10 specification as recorded in the
# reference pack. **Width 1.350 m and height 1.594 m are estimates from the
# photographs**, not published figures: the width from the wing-to-wing span
# relative to the body in the rear photograph and the height from the upright
# screen relative to the wheel diameter and body. They are recorded as
# estimates and must not be quoted as specifications.
#
# Shape reference: the 1919 car's front three-quarter and rear quarter. **The
# pack's image 1 is a 1924 period side illustration of the coupe body**, a
# different body from the open two-seater, and was used only for the wheelbase
# and bonnet proportions; the body section is the photographed car's. The model
# carries the right-hand-drive layout, the spare wheel on the right running
# board, the rear auxiliary seat, flat wings and the tall narrow bonnet.
#
# Colours: these cars were finished to the buyer's order and no British colour
# list exists. The objects carry plain period-appropriate names: green, black,
# maroon and blue. Weights green 3, black 3, maroon 2, blue 2, total 10,
# provisional pending the central rebase. Brass, nickel, tyres, seats and the
# folded hood keep their own materials.
#
# Speed 80 km/h, an estimate for a 1919 10 hp light car; no published maximum
# was found and the figure is recorded as an estimate. Comfort 35/235, on the
# line 180 + 1.5 x 35: an open car with a folding screen. Classes 1,2.
#
# Dates are road presence: 1912 intro, retires staggered 1929-1932 on a
# seventeen- to twenty-year tail.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/singer-ten.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/singer-ten.dae (146 objects). References:
# citycars/references/singer-ten/ (gitignored; Wikimedia Commons).
#
# Accepted deviations: the bonnet and scuttle are more faceted than the
# photographs, the front lamps are partly obscured in the front-quarter view,
# and the windscreen is represented by its frame without a pane. At 128 px the
# tall narrow bonnet, the open seats and the flat wings read as the Singer Ten.

